= Ideal magnetohydrodynamics
{title2=ideal MHD}
{wiki}

= Ideal MHD
{synonym}

Ideal magnetohydrodynamics assumes vanishing resistivity, so magnetic flux is frozen into the fluid and the electric field in the fluid rest frame vanishes.

= Magnetic relaxation
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

Magnetic relaxation converts <magnetic energy> into material motion through the <Lorentz force density>, and dissipates the resulting <kinetic energy> through <viscosity>. In a <perfect conductor>, <magnetic flux freezing> preserves field-line connectivity throughout every smooth stage. A regular limiting state has zero <velocity> and is a <magnetostatic equilibrium>, but its <magnetic field> can contain <current sheets>. Conservation of one global <magnetic helicity> is weaker than preservation of the complete frozen field-line structure.

= Single-molecule magnet
{parent=Magnetic relaxation}

A finite molecular <spin> system exhibiting slow reversal of its magnetic moment on an experimental timescale. Blocking is timescale-dependent and does not require bulk long-range magnetic order.

= Quantum tunnelling of magnetization
{parent=Magnetic relaxation}

Reversal between opposite magnetic orientations through quantum mixing rather than activation over the full classical anisotropy barrier.

= Collapse of a planar magnetic separatrix
{parent=Magnetic relaxation}

For a planar <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=(\psi_y,-\psi_x,0)$, ideal induction advects the <Cartesian magnetic flux function>: $D_t\psi=0$. <Incompressible flow> also preserves the area inside each material contour. During relaxation, the arms of a magnetic <separatrix> can approach each other while their field-line connectivity remains fixed. A transition of width $\epsilon$ with a finite change of tangential field has <electric current density> of order $\epsilon^{-1}$ and can approach a <current sheet>. This is a possible limiting mechanism, not a claim that every planar equilibrium is singular.

= Viscous magnetic-relaxation energy identity
{parent=Magnetic relaxation}
{title2=$\dot E=-\rho\nu\int_D|\nabla\times\mathbf v|^2\,dV$}

For constant <mass density> $\rho$, <kinematic viscosity> $\nu>0$, ideal induction, zero boundary <velocity> and tangent <magnetic field>, put $E=\int_D[\rho|\mathbf v|^2/2+|\mathbf B|^2/(2\mu_0)]dV$. Multiplying the momentum equation by $\mathbf v$ and integrating cancels pressure work and advective flux. The magnetic-energy derivative is minus the integral of <Lorentz force density> dotted with <velocity>, canceling the same work in the kinetic-energy equation. <Integration by parts> then gives the displayed identity. For <incompressible flow> with zero boundary velocity, $\int|\nabla\mathbf v|^2=\int|\nabla\times\mathbf v|^2$.

= Steady planar ideal-MHD field-line invariants
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}
{title2=$\mathbf u=k\mathbf B/\rho+U\mathbf e_z$}

For a stationary, $z$-independent <ideal magnetohydrodynamics> flow with $E_z=0$, nonzero horizontal <magnetic field> and nonzero <magnetohydrodynamic mass loading> $k$, the displayed decomposition has $\mathbf B\cdot\nabla k=\mathbf B\cdot\nabla U=0$. The <continuity equation> gives the first invariant, and the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> gives the second. Entropy and $V=u_z-B_z/(\mu_0k)$ are also constant on each <magnetic field line>. Stationarity alone permits constant nonzero $E_z$, so it does not imply this decomposition.

= Alfvénic degeneracy of steady planar MHD
{c}
{parent=Steady planar ideal-MHD field-line invariants}
{title2=$B_z(\mu_0k^2/\rho-1)=\mu_0k(V-U)$}

The two axial invariants in <steady planar ideal-MHD field-line invariants> give the displayed identity. When $V-U\ne0$, a finite smooth flow cannot reach horizontal <Alfvén speed>. When $V=U$, an everywhere Alfvénic branch is possible even with $B_z\ne0$: constant $\rho$ and $\mathbf u=\mathbf B/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}$ give an example. The nondegenerate exclusion must not be applied to this branch.

= Planar ideal-MHD Bernoulli invariant
{parent=Steady planar ideal-MHD field-line invariants}
{title2=$Q=u^2/2+\Phi+w-UB_z/(\mu_0k)$}

Under the hypotheses of <steady planar ideal-MHD field-line invariants>, the <specific enthalpy> relation and the velocity projection of momentum give $\mathbf B\cdot\nabla Q=0$. The horizontal total energy flux, including the <Poynting vector>, is $k\mathbf B_h Q$. The magnetic correction accounts for work exchanged between the fluid and the field.

= Grad-Shafranov equation
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}
{c}
{wiki=Grad–Shafranov_equation}

The Grad-Shafranov equation governs the poloidal magnetic flux of an axisymmetric magnetohydrostatic equilibrium. Pressure and toroidal-field contributions depend on the flux function. The <Grad-Shafranov equation for a force-free magnetic field> is its zero-pressure-gradient reduction.

= Grad-Shafranov equation for a force-free magnetic field
{c}
{parent=Grad-Shafranov equation}

For an axisymmetric force-free field with $\mathbf B_p=\nabla\psi\times\nabla\phi$ and $rB_\phi=F(\psi)$, the flux function obeys
$$
-r\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left(\frac1r\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial r}\right)
-\frac{\partial^2\psi}{\partial z^2}
=F\frac{dF}{d\psi}.
$$

= Reduced magnetohydrodynamics
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}
{title2=$k_\parallel/k_\perp\ll1$}

= Reduced MHD
{synonym}

= RMHD
{synonym}
{c}

<Reduced magnetohydrodynamics> is the leading ideal-fluid description of small, anisotropic fluctuations about a strong uniform guide field, with $k_\parallel/k_\perp\sim\delta B_\perp/B_0\sim\delta u_\perp/v_A\ll1$. Its <solenoidal> perpendicular <Elsässer variables> obey
$$
\partial_t\mathbf z^\pm_\perp\mp v_A\partial_z\mathbf z^\pm_\perp+(\mathbf z^\mp_\perp\cdot\nabla_\perp)\mathbf z^\pm_\perp=-\nabla_\perp\Pi.
$$
The parallel magnetic derivative includes bending of field lines: $\nabla_\parallel=\partial_z+(\delta\mathbf B_\perp/B_0)\cdot\nabla_\perp$. Slow compressive and <entropy mode> fluctuations are passively transported at this order. The system excludes the rapidly propagating <fast magnetosonic wave> sector rather than describing all possible <MHD> perturbations.

= Five quadratic cascade invariants of anisotropic magnetohydrodynamic turbulence
{parent=Reduced magnetohydrodynamics}
{title2=$I_A^+,I_A^-,I_S^+,I_S^-,I_e$}

For ideal <reduced magnetohydrodynamics> with periodic or vanishing-flux boundaries, the five quadratic cascade channels are $I_A^\pm=\int|\mathbf z_\perp^\pm|^2dV$, $I_S^\pm=\int(f^\pm)^2dV$ and $I_e=\int s^2dV$. The perpendicular <Elsässer variables>, slow-wave amplitudes $f^\pm$, and <entropy> fluctuation $s$ each obey an equation with <divergence-free> advection. Multiplying each equation by its own field and integrating makes both advection and propagation terms boundary fluxes, while the perpendicular <pressure> term vanishes by solenoidality. Each integral is therefore separately conserved. The two Alfvén cascades distort one another without exchanging their integrated invariants; the other three are passively cascaded by the Alfvénic motion without feedback at this order. This count concerns quadratic fluctuation-energy channels: an advected scalar also conserves arbitrary integrals $\int F(s)dV$, which are not extra independent quadratic modal channels.

= Slow and entropy fluctuations in reduced magnetohydrodynamics
{parent=Reduced magnetohydrodynamics}
{title2=$Df^\pm=\mp c_T\nabla_\parallel f^\pm$}

In ideal <reduced magnetohydrodynamics> with constant background sound and <Alfvén speeds>, perpendicular total-pressure balance gives $\delta p+\rho_0v_A^2\delta B_\parallel/B_0=0$. Put $s=\delta p/p_0-\gamma\delta\rho/\rho_0$, $r=\delta\rho/\rho_0+s/\gamma$, $c_T=v_Ac_s/(v_A^2+c_s^2)^{1/2}$ and $h=c_s^2r/c_T$. Then the parallel momentum, continuity and induction equations yield
$$
Du_\parallel=-c_T\nabla_\parallel h,\qquad Dh=-c_T\nabla_\parallel u_\parallel,\qquad Ds=0.
$$
Thus $f^\pm=u_\parallel\pm h$ satisfy $Df^\pm=\mp c_T\nabla_\parallel f^\pm$. The two slow-wave populations propagate along bent <magnetic field lines> and are mixed by the perpendicular <Alfvénic turbulence>, while the <entropy> fluctuation is simply advected. They do not force the perpendicular Alfvénic system at this leading order.

= Ideal magnetohydrodynamic energy conservation
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}
{title2=$\partial_t\mathcal E+\nabla\cdot\mathbf F=0$}

For a fixed <Newtonian gravitational potential>, total <energy density> is $\mathcal E=\rho(u^2/2+\Phi)+p/(\gamma-1)+B^2/(2\mu_0)$. Its flux combines advected kinetic/potential energy, <specific enthalpy> and the <Poynting vector>. Dotting the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic momentum equation> with <velocity>, adding the adiabatic <internal energy> equation and the <magnetic energy> equation cancels the magnetic work. The <pressure> terms combine into $-\nabla\cdot(p\mathbf u)$, proving the conservation law. A time-dependent imposed potential instead contributes $\rho\partial_t\Phi$.

= Ideal magnetohydrodynamic momentum equation
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}
{title2=$\rho D_t\mathbf u=-\nabla p-\rho\nabla\Phi+(\nabla\times\mathbf B)\times\mathbf B/\mu_0$}

The inviscid <magnetohydrodynamic momentum equation> balances inertia, <pressure>, <gravitational acceleration> and <Lorentz force density>. The magnetic force separates into <magnetic tension> and <magnetic pressure>. It is coupled to the <continuity equation> and the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation>.

= Plane-parallel magnetohydrodynamic flow with imposed shear
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

A useful <ideal magnetohydrodynamics> reduction has $\mathbf u=\mathbf v(z,t)+ax\mathbf e_y$, $\mathbf B=\mathbf B(z,t)$ and $\rho=\rho(z,t)$. The constant background <shear flow> supplies $av_x$ in the axial momentum equation and $aB_x$ in axial magnetic induction. The solenoidal constraint and <MHD induction equation> together make $B_z$ constant.

= Alfvén-point compatibility in a plane-parallel sheared flow
{parent=Plane-parallel magnetohydrodynamic flow with imposed shear}
{c}

At $w^2=v_{az}^2$, the transverse steady equations require $wB_xw'=0$ and $wB_yw'=a(wB_x-B_zv_x)$. These conditions can be lost by division when eliminating transverse magnetic derivatives. A scalar equation displaying the <magnetosonic critical speeds> need not display every compatibility condition at an <Alfvén speed>.

= Magnetohydrodynamic shear work
{parent=Plane-parallel magnetohydrodynamic flow with imposed shear}
{title2=$S=a(B_xB_y/\mu_0-\rho v_xv_y)$}

The energy of the residual flow and <magnetic field> in a maintained <shear flow> has source $S=-aT_{xy}$, where $T_{xy}=\rho v_xv_y-B_xB_y/\mu_0$. The kinetic and magnetic stresses can exchange energy with the imposed background. The source vanishes when their off-diagonal contributions cancel.

= Ideal magnetohydrodynamic equations
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}
{title2=$\partial_t\mathbf U+A_i(\mathbf U)\partial_i\mathbf U=0$}

The smooth ideal-MHD system couples <mass conservation>, <momentum>, adiabatic <pressure> transport and the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation>, subject to the <solenoidal magnetic-field constraint>. Without gravity it is a <quasilinear partial differential equation> system. Across shocks, conservative total energy replaces smooth adiabatic <pressure> transport.

= Magnetohydrodynamic energy principle
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

For a static smooth <ideal magnetohydrodynamics> equilibrium with conservative external forces and surface terms eliminated by the <boundary conditions>, the linear displacement equation is $\rho\ddot{\boldsymbol\xi}=\mathcal F\boldsymbol\xi$, where the force operator is a <self-adjoint operator> in the appropriate integral pairing. Its restoring <quadratic form> $\delta W=-\tfrac12\int\boldsymbol\xi^*\cdot\mathcal F\boldsymbol\xi\,dV$ is real. Multiplying the <normal mode> equation by the <complex conjugate> of the <displacement> and integrating gives $\omega^2\int\rho|\boldsymbol\xi|^2dV=2\delta W$. Hence squared frequencies are real, and a negative restoring form gives an unstable mode through the variational <Rayleigh quotient>, subject to the operator domain and spectral hypotheses. Background <fluid flow> adds terms to this argument; the static hypothesis is essential.

= Incompressible magnetic displacement energy identity
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic energy principle}
{title2=$\sigma^2\int\rho|\xi|^2=\int\xi_i^*\xi_j(\Pi_{,ij}+\rho\Phi_{,ij})+\mu_0^{-1}\int|(B\cdot\nabla)\xi|^2$}

For a static <ideal magnetohydrodynamics> equilibrium in fixed gravity, let $\nabla\cdot\xi=0$ and $\Pi=p+B^2/(2\mu_0)$. Frozen flux gives $b=(B\cdot\nabla)\xi-(\xi\cdot\nabla)B$ and continuity gives $\rho'=-\xi\cdot\nabla\rho$. Multiplying the linear momentum equation by the conjugate displacement and integrating by parts gives the displayed identity when surface terms vanish. The two magnetic cross terms cancel; the remaining magnetic equilibrium derivative combines with the <mass density>-gradient force to give the two <Hessian matrices>. Real symmetric <Hessian matrices> and the nonnegative <magnetic tension> term make $\sigma^2$ real.

= Interchange stability of an incompressible magnetized atmosphere
{parent=Incompressible magnetic displacement energy identity}
{title2=$Q=-g\int\rho_z|\xi_z|^2+\mu_0^{-1}\int B^2|\partial_x\xi|^2$}

For a horizontal field $B(z)e_x$ in constant downward gravity, equilibrium obeys $(p+B^2/(2\mu_0))_z=-\rho g$. The displacement energy reduces to the displayed <quadratic form>. Perturbations independent of the field direction do not bend <magnetic field lines> and therefore have no <magnetic tension> penalty: an increasing <mass density> with height permits instability. A nonincreasing <mass density> gives nonnegative energy for all admitted incompressible perturbations. Along-field variation adds <magnetic tension> proportional to <wavenumber> squared, stabilizing sufficiently short waves when buoyancy is unfavorable.

= Linear displacement equations for a magnetized atmosphere
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic energy principle}
{title2=$\delta\rho=-\nabla\cdot(\rho\xi),\quad\delta B=\nabla\times(\xi\times B)$}

For a static equilibrium and a <fluid Lagrangian displacement> $\xi$, <conservation of mass>, adiabatic <entropy> and <magnetic flux freezing> give $\delta p=-\xi\cdot\nabla p-\gamma p\nabla\cdot\xi$ together with the displayed relations. Linearizing the <pressure>/tension form of the momentum equation yields $\rho\ddot\xi=-\nabla\delta\Pi-g\delta\rho e_z+\mu_0^{-1}(\delta B\cdot\nabla B+B\cdot\nabla\delta B)$, where $\delta\Pi=\delta p+B\cdot\delta B/\mu_0$. Static force balance removes the zeroth-order terms; the <density> multiplying acceleration is the equilibrium <density>.

= Elsässer variable
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf z^\pm$}

For an <incompressible flow> with constant <mass density>, the <Elsässer variables> are $\mathbf z^\pm=\mathbf u\pm\mathbf v_a$, where $\mathbf v_a$ is the <Alfvén velocity>. Adding and subtracting the <Euler momentum equation> and <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> yields $\partial_t\mathbf z^\pm+(\mathbf z^\mp\cdot\nabla)\mathbf z^\pm=-\nabla\psi$, with $\psi=p/\rho+\Phi+|\mathbf v_a|^2/2$ and $\nabla\cdot\mathbf z^\pm=0$. Thus each field is transported by the other, a useful way to expose interactions of oppositely directed <Alfvén waves>.

= Elsässer energy invariant
{parent=Elsässer variable}
{c}
{title2=$I_\pm$}

For smooth <ideal magnetohydrodynamics> of constant <mass density>, define $I_\pm=\int_V|\mathbf z^\pm|^2dV$. Taking the <dot product> of the <Elsässer variable> equation with $2\mathbf z^\pm$ gives
$$
\partial_t|\mathbf z^\pm|^2+\nabla\cdot\left(|\mathbf z^\pm|^2\mathbf z^\mp+2\psi\mathbf z^\pm\right)=0.
$$
Therefore each integral is constant when its outward boundary flux vanishes, for example when both <velocity> and <magnetic field> are tangent to the fixed boundary, or for <periodic boundary conditions>. The <kinetic energy> plus <magnetic energy> is $\rho(I_++I_-)/4$, while the <cross-helicity> is $\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}(I_+-I_-)/4$.

= Cross-helicity
{title2=$H_c=\int\mathbf u\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B\,dV$}
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

Cross-helicity measures alignment between velocity and magnetic field in a magnetized fluid. Its density is $h_c=\mathbf u\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B$.

= Cross-helicity conservation law
{parent=Cross-helicity}

Let $h_c=\mathbf u\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B$ be the <cross-helicity> density. The <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> implies $D\mathbf B/Dt=(\mathbf B\mathbin\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u-\mathbf B\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf u$. Dotting the momentum equation with $\mathbf B$ eliminates the <Lorentz force density>, since it is perpendicular to the <magnetic field>. The <material derivative> of $h_c$ is therefore
$$
\frac{Dh_c}{Dt}
=\mathbf B\mathbin\cdot\nabla\left(\frac{u^2}{2}-\Phi\right)
-\frac{\mathbf B\mathbin\cdot\nabla p}{\rho}
-h_c\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf u.
$$
Using $dh=T\,ds+dp/\rho$ for <specific enthalpy> $h$ and <specific entropy> $s$, together with <Gauss's law for magnetism>, converts this to
$$
\partial_th_c+\nabla\mathbin\cdot\left[
\mathbf u h_c+\mathbf B\left(h+\Phi-\frac{u^2}{2}\right)
\right]
=T\mathbf B\mathbin\cdot\nabla s.
$$
The source vanishes when <magnetic field lines> lie in constant-<specific entropy> surfaces, in particular in a <homentropic flow>. Integrating over a volume conserves its total <cross-helicity> only if the boundary flux also vanishes. This local law is derived in https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.03835[Ogilvie's astrophysical fluid dynamics notes].

= Material conservation of cross-helicity density
{parent=Cross-helicity conservation law}

In a <homentropic flow>, the <cross-helicity conservation law> gives
$$
\frac{Dh_c}{Dt}
=\mathbf B\mathbin\cdot\nabla\left(\frac{u^2}{2}-h-\Phi\right)
-h_c\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf u.
$$
Consequently <cross-helicity> density is conserved along each trajectory exactly when the right-hand side vanishes. In a <steady state>, <mass conservation> gives $\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf u=-\mathbf u\mathbin\cdot\nabla\log\rho$, so the criterion becomes
$$
\mathbf B\mathbin\cdot\nabla\left(h+\Phi-\frac{u^2}{2}\right)
=(\mathbf u\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B)\mathbf u\mathbin\cdot\nabla\log\rho.
$$
The absence of an <entropy> source in a conservation law does not alone make its density an advected scalar: compression and transport along the <magnetic field> still change that density.

= Magnetic flux freezing
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

Magnetic flux freezing states that every material loop in an ideal conducting fluid encloses constant magnetic flux. A deformation perpendicular to a uniform field that preserves mass per unit length therefore preserves $B/\rho$.

= Material magnetic-flux transport theorem
{parent=Magnetic flux freezing}
{title2=$d\Phi_B/dt=\int_{S(t)}[\partial_t\mathbf B-\nabla\times(\mathbf u\times\mathbf B)+\mathbf u\nabla\cdot\mathbf B]\cdot d\mathbf S$}

Combine the <material surface element evolution> law with the <product rule> for $\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S$. The result is
$$
\frac d{dt}\int_{S(t)}\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S
=\int_{S(t)}\left[\frac{D\mathbf B}{Dt}+\mathbf B\nabla\cdot\mathbf u-(\mathbf B\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u\right]\cdot d\mathbf S.
$$
Expanding the <curl> gives the equivalent title formula. The <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> and <Gauss's law for magnetism> make the integrand zero and prove <magnetic flux freezing>, without requiring <incompressible flow>.

= Flux-surface preservation during magnetic-tube expansion
{parent=Magnetic flux freezing}

Suppose the <Cartesian magnetic flux function> is advected, the initial axial velocity is zero, $B_y=f(\psi)$ initially, and $\nabla\cdot\mathbf u=g(\psi,t)$. Then $B_y=f(\psi)\exp[-\int_0^t g(\psi,s)\,ds]$ remains a function of the flux label and time. The axial <Lorentz force> vanishes because its transverse derivative along a <magnetic field line> vanishes. Smooth ideal evolution therefore preserves zero axial motion.

= Mass-to-flux ratio
{title2=$M/\Phi_B$}
{parent=Magnetic flux freezing}

The mass-to-flux ratio is the matter carried per unit magnetic flux by a bundle of field lines. Ideal magnetohydrodynamics preserves it for a material flux tube.

= Critical mass-to-flux ratio
{parent=Mass-to-flux ratio}
{title2=$(M/\Phi)_{\rm crit}=\lambda/\sqrt{\mu_0G}$}

For fixed cloud shape, gravitational energy scales as $-C_gGM^2/L$ and <magnetic energy> as $C_B\Phi^2/(\mu_0L)$. The magnetic-criticality threshold for negligible <pressure> is $M/\Phi>\sqrt{C_B/C_g}/\sqrt{\mu_0G}$. Ideal flux freezing keeps this ratio fixed during homologous collapse; geometry determines the numerical coefficient.

= Alfvén speed
{title2=$v_A$}
{c}
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

The Alfvén speed associated with magnetic-field magnitude $B$ and density $\rho$ is $v_A=B/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}$.

= Alfvén Mach number
{c}
{parent=Alfvén speed}
{title2=$M_A=u/v_A$}

The Alfvén Mach number compares a characteristic flow speed with the <Alfvén speed> $v_A=B/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}$. In an <axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind>, the relevant poloidal quantity is $M_{Ap}=|\mathbf u_p|\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}/|\mathbf B_p|$. With <magnetohydrodynamic mass loading> $\rho\mathbf u_p=\kappa\mathbf B_p$, its square is $\mu_0\kappa^2/\rho$. The <Alfvén surface> is the locus $M_{Ap}=1$, and a smooth crossing requires the corresponding <Alfvén-surface regularity condition for an axisymmetric wind>. The total-speed and poloidal-speed definitions should be distinguished when there is substantial rotation or a strong <toroidal magnetic field>.

= Alfvén velocity
{parent=Alfvén speed}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf v_a$}

The <Alfvén velocity> is the signed vector $\mathbf v_a=\mathbf B/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}$, whose magnitude is the <Alfvén speed>. It follows the orientation of the <magnetic field>, unlike the nonnegative scalar <speed>.

= Alfvén number
{title2=$A$}
{c}
{parent=Alfvén speed}

An Alfvén number is the ratio of a flow speed to the corresponding Alfvén speed. For a poloidal flow parallel to a poloidal magnetic field, $A^2=\mu_0\rho u_p^2/B_p^2$.

= Magnetohydrodynamic wave
{title2=MHD wave}
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

A magnetohydrodynamic wave is a linear disturbance of a conducting fluid and its magnetic field. A uniform ideal plasma supports an Alfvén mode and fast and slow magnetosonic modes.

= Parallel-propagating ideal magnetohydrodynamic wave
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic wave}
{title2=$\omega^2=k^2v_A^2\ \text{or}\ k^2c_s^2$}

In a uniform ideal-MHD <plasma> at rest, take the <wavevector> parallel to a nonzero background field. Projecting the linear <velocity> equation along and perpendicular to the field gives two transverse Alfvén <polarizations> at speed $v_A$ and one longitudinal acoustic <polarization> at speed $c_s$. Compression does not bend field lines for the longitudinal branch. The general oblique fast/slow magnetosonic modes reduce to the corresponding faster/slower parallel branches, with degeneracies when their speeds agree.

= Ambipolar damping
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic wave}

<Ambipolar damping> is attenuation of magnetic disturbances by friction between the magnetically coupled charged fluid and a neutral fluid. In a strongly collisional mixture, the fluids nearly move together, but their small relative <velocity> dissipates <energy>. With drag coefficient $\rho_i\mu_{in}=\rho_n\mu_{ni}$, the frictional <energy> loss is $-\rho_i\mu_{in}\int|\mathbf u_i-\mathbf u_n|^2dV$. Neglecting other dissipative processes does not remove this collisional loss.

= Equal-density ion-neutral Alfvén dispersion relation
{parent=Ambipolar damping}
{title2=$\omega^3+2i\mu\omega^2-a^2\omega-i\mu a^2=0$}

For equal <ion> and neutral mass densities, let $\mu=\mu_{in}=\mu_{ni}$, $a=k_\parallel B_0/\sqrt{4\pi\rho_i}$ and use Fourier time dependence $e^{-i\omega t}$. <Solenoidal> <displacements> obey $\ddot\xi_i=-a^2\xi_i-\mu(\dot\xi_i-\dot\xi_n)$ and $\ddot\xi_n=-\mu(\dot\xi_n-\dot\xi_i)$. For nonzero <frequency>, the neutral equation gives $\xi_i=(1-i\omega/\mu)\xi_n$. Substitution in the <ion> equation gives
$$
\omega^3+2i\mu\omega^2-a^2\omega-i\mu a^2=0.
$$
Equivalently, the <growth rate> $s=-i\omega$ obeys $s^3+2\mu s^2+a^2s+\mu a^2=0$. A time-independent neutral <displacement> with zero <velocity> is a relabeling of a uniform fluid, not an additional <wave> root of the <velocity>/magnetic-field system.

= Strong-collision ion-neutral Alfvén modes
{parent=Equal-density ion-neutral Alfvén dispersion relation}
{title2=$\omega_\pm=\pm a/\sqrt2-ia^2/(8\mu)$}

For $|a|\ll\mu$, the equal-density ion-neutral cubic has a rapidly damped mode $\omega_d=-2i\mu+ia^2/(4\mu)+O(a^4/\mu^3)$ and two propagating modes $\omega_\pm=\pm a/\sqrt2-ia^2/(8\mu)+O(a^3/\mu^2)$. The reduced <phase speed> uses the total <density> $\rho_i+\rho_n=2\rho_i$. The exact <displacement> ratio is $\xi_n/\xi_i=\mu/(\mu-i\omega)$. It is nearly $-1$ for the rapidly damped counterflow and nearly $1\pm ia/(\sqrt2\mu)$ for the propagating modes. Thus the latter have small ion-neutral slippage of relative size $|a|/\mu$, responsible for <ambipolar damping>.

= Entropy mode
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic wave}
{title2=$Ds=0$}

An <entropy mode> is a perturbation of <entropy> and <density> with no leading <pressure> or <velocity> perturbation in a uniform ideal fluid. For an <adiabatic equation of state> define $s=\delta p/p_0-\gamma\delta\rho/\rho_0$. An <entropy> perturbation can have $\delta p=0$ and $\delta\rho/\rho_0=-s/\gamma$, so it has zero <frequency> in the local fluid frame. In anisotropic <reduced magnetohydrodynamics>, $Ds=0$ with $D=\partial_t+\mathbf u_\perp\cdot\nabla_\perp$, and <turbulence> mixes its <variance> as a <passive scalar>.

= Magnetohydrodynamic wave polarization
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic wave}
{title2=$\mathbf u'=U\widehat{\mathbf e}$}

= MHD wave polarization
{c}
{synonym}

A polarization specifies the direction or trajectory of the oscillating <velocity> and <magnetic field> in a <magnetohydrodynamic wave>. The linear <Alfvén wave> has velocity perpendicular to the plane of the background <magnetic field> and <wavevector>; the <magnetosonic waves> have velocity in that plane. A real fixed polarization vector gives a linear oscillation. Two orthogonal components of equal amplitude in quadrature give a circular trajectory. For propagation parallel to a uniform field, combining the two degenerate transverse linear polarizations produces circular polarization; at finite amplitude its constant transverse magnetic-field magnitude removes the longitudinal <magnetic pressure> gradient.

= Uniform-current rotating magnetohydrodynamic wave dispersion
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic wave}
{title2=$\sigma=i\delta\Omega\pm\sqrt{J^2(2m\delta-m^2)/(4\mu_0\rho)-\delta^2\Omega^2}$}

For an ideal <incompressible flow> rotating about the axis of the field $\mathbf B=(J/2)s\widehat{\boldsymbol\phi}$, use cylindrical <Fourier modes> $e^{\sigma t+im\phi}$. The <azimuthal derivative of a cylindrical vector Fourier mode> reduces induction to $\sigma\mathbf b=imJ\mathbf u/2$. If the boundary eigenproblem gives $\lambda=i\delta\nu$, with real $|\delta|<1$, eliminating the perturbation gives the displayed <dispersion relation>. Here $J=\nabla\times\mathbf B\cdot\widehat{\mathbf z}=\mu_0J_{\rm phys}$ for SI <current density>; the formula is conditional on that boundary eigenrelation.

= Single-azimuthal-mode current-driven instability threshold
{parent=Uniform-current rotating magnetohydrodynamic wave dispersion}
{title2=$|m|=1,\quad m\delta>\tfrac12$}

In the <uniform-current rotating magnetohydrodynamic wave dispersion>, exponential growth requires $J^2(2m\delta-m^2)/(4\mu_0\rho)>\delta^2\Omega^2$. For integer $m$ and $|\delta|<1$ this is possible only for $|m|=1$ and $m\delta>1/2$. Positive and negative $m$ branches are related by <complex conjugation>. The equality case is a repeated frequency and requires separate analysis of possible algebraic growth.

= Alfvén wave
{c}
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic wave}
{wiki}

= Shear Alfvén wave
{synonym}
{c}

An <Alfvén wave> is a <magnetohydrodynamic wave> in which <magnetic tension> restores a sideways disturbance of a conducting <fluid>. In the small-<amplitude>, uniform-background <ideal magnetohydrodynamics> model, its shear <polarization of waves> has <displacement> perpendicular to both the background <magnetic field> and the <wavevector>. It is an <incompressible flow> disturbance: the <mass density> and gas <pressure> have no first-order fluctuation. Its <dispersion relation> is $\omega^2=(\mathbf k\mathbin\cdot\mathbf v_A)^2$, where $\mathbf v_A$ is the background <Alfvén velocity>.

The mechanism resembles a plucked taut string: <magnetic flux freezing> makes sideways <fluid flow> bend <magnetic field lines>, and their <magnetic tension> accelerates neighboring fluid. The fluid supplies inertia and the bent <magnetic field> supplies the restoring stress. This permits <energy> transport without a first-order compression, unlike an <acoustic wave>. <Hannes Alfvén>'s 1942 paper, Existence of Electromagnetic-Hydrodynamic Waves, considered precisely a uniform axial <magnetic field>, infinite <electrical conductivity> and sideways <velocity> depending on axial position and time; it proposed a solar application. This was a theoretical prediction, followed by the distinct <Lundquist liquid-metal Alfvén wave experiment>.<Existence of Electromagnetic-Hydrodynamic Waves by Hannes Olof Gösta Alfvén>[1942, pp. 405–406; ideal-conductor plane-wave argument, reproduced on slide 4 of Kinetic Alfvén waves in space plasmas]{ref}

\Image[alfven-wave-magnetic-tension.png]
{title=Magnetic tension restores a transverse Alfvén-wave displacement}
{description=An original schematic of a small sideways bend in a uniform background <magnetic field>. The <displacement> and restoring <Lorentz force density> point in opposite directions at the crest; the transverse <magnetic field> follows the slope, so it vanishes at that crest. With <fluid Lagrangian displacement> $\boldsymbol\xi$, <magnetic flux freezing> gives $\mathbf b_\perp=B_0\partial_z\boldsymbol\xi_\perp$. The drawing exaggerates the bend for readability.}

For oblique propagation, the shear <Alfvén wave> has <velocity> normal to the plane containing the <wavevector> and background <magnetic field>. The <fast magnetosonic wave> and <slow magnetosonic wave> instead have <velocity> in that plane and generally involve compression, with gas <pressure> and <magnetic pressure> contributing to their restoring forces. For exactly parallel propagation the plane is not unique: either transverse <polarization of waves> is allowed, and some modal speeds coincide. For $k_\parallel=0$ the shear branch has $\omega=0$, while the <fast magnetosonic wave> can still propagate perpendicular to the <magnetic field>. One should identify the <polarization of waves> as well as the <phase velocity>. The statement that the shear mode is incompressible concerns this linear mode; a finite linearly polarized disturbance can drive a quadratic compressive response, as in the <magnetic-pressure obstruction to a linearly polarized Alfvén wave>.

= Linear Alfvén wave equations
{c}
{parent=Alfvén wave}
{title2=$\omega=\pm k_\parallel v_A$}

Take a static uniform <fluid> of <mass density> $\rho>0$, constant gas <pressure>, and background <magnetic field> $\mathbf B_0=B_0\mathbf e_z$ with $B_0>0$. Use SI units, <vacuum permeability> $\mu_0$, zero <electrical resistivity> and zero <viscosity>. Write $\mathbf B=\mathbf B_0+\mathbf b_\perp(z,t)$ and $\mathbf u=\mathbf u_\perp(z,t)$, with both disturbances perpendicular to $\mathbf e_z$. Retain only <linear order> in their <amplitudes>. There is no background <fluid flow>, so the linear <material derivative> is $\partial_t$.

The <continuity equation> has $\nabla\cdot\mathbf u_\perp=0$, hence $\partial_t\delta\rho=0$; choosing no initial <mass density> fluctuation gives $\delta\rho=0$. For this shear mode the gas <pressure> fluctuation is also zero. In the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic momentum equation>, the <Lorentz force density> can be written
$$
\frac{(\nabla\times\mathbf B)\times\mathbf B}{\mu_0}
=\frac{(\mathbf B\cdot\nabla)\mathbf B}{\mu_0}
-\nabla\frac{B^2}{2\mu_0}.
$$
Because $\mathbf B_0\cdot\mathbf b_\perp=0$, the perturbed <magnetic pressure> starts at quadratic order. The surviving transverse <magnetic tension> gives the first equation below. Expanding the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> gives the second:
$$
\rho\,\partial_t\mathbf u_\perp=\frac{B_0}{\mu_0}\partial_z\mathbf b_\perp,
\qquad
\partial_t\mathbf b_\perp=B_0\partial_z\mathbf u_\perp.
$$
Differentiate the first in time and substitute the second. Both <velocity> and <magnetic field> disturbances obey the <one-dimensional wave equation>:
$$
\partial_t^2\mathbf u_\perp=v_A^2\partial_z^2\mathbf u_\perp,
\qquad v_A=\frac{B_0}{\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}}.
$$
For a <plane wave> proportional to $e^{i(k_\parallel z-\omega t)}$, this gives $\omega^2=k_\parallel^2v_A^2$, or $\omega=\pm k_\parallel v_A$. The magnetic and <velocity> <amplitudes> satisfy $\mathbf b_\perp=-(B_0k_\parallel/\omega)\mathbf u_\perp$: they have opposite signs for propagation along $+\mathbf e_z$ and equal signs for propagation along $-\mathbf e_z$. The general uniform-background shear <polarization of waves>, perpendicular to both $\mathbf k$ and $\mathbf B_0$, gives the same <dispersion relation> with $k_\parallel=\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf e_z$. Its <group velocity> is $\pm v_A\mathbf e_z$, whereas its <phase velocity> normal to an oblique wavefront has magnitude $v_A|k_\parallel|/|\mathbf k|$.

= Alfvén wave energy transport
{c}
{parent=Alfvén wave}
{title2=$\partial_t\mathcal E+\partial_zF_z=0$}

Dot the two <linear Alfvén wave equations> with $\mathbf u_\perp$ and $\mathbf b_\perp/\mu_0$, respectively, and add. The result is <conservation of energy> with
$$
\mathcal E=\frac{\rho|\mathbf u_\perp|^2}{2}+\frac{|\mathbf b_\perp|^2}{2\mu_0},
\qquad F_z=-\frac{B_0}{\mu_0}\mathbf u_\perp\cdot\mathbf b_\perp.
$$
A single traveling <Alfvén wave> has equal <kinetic energy> and perturbation <magnetic energy>, and $F_z=\pm v_A\mathcal E$. This equality need not hold instantaneously at each point of a superposed <standing wave>.

As an original ideal-model example, stipulate $B_0=1.00\,\mathrm T$, $\rho=10^4\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$ and a uniform path $L=0.20\,\mathrm m$. Using $\mu_0\simeq4\pi\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{H\,m^{-1}}$, the <Alfvén speed> is $8.92\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and the transit time $L/v_A$ is $22.4\,\mathrm{ms}$. Doubling the <magnetic field> halves that time. A sinusoidal <velocity> with peak <amplitude> $0.010\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ has magnetic peak <amplitude> $1.12\,\mathrm{mT}$ and mean <energy> flux $\langle F_z\rangle=\rho v_AU^2/2=4.46\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$ in the positive direction. These are stipulated ideal parameters, not historical measurements or a prediction including vessel walls.

= Resistive damping of an Alfvén wave
{c}
{parent=Alfvén wave}
{title2=$\omega^2+i\eta_m k_\parallel^2\omega-v_A^2k_\parallel^2=0$}

Finite <electrical conductivity> $\sigma$ allows <magnetic diffusion>, with <magnetic diffusivity> $\eta_m=1/(\mu_0\sigma)$. In the same uniform, unbounded geometry as the <linear Alfvén wave equations>, neglect <viscosity> but add $\eta_m\partial_z^2\mathbf b_\perp$ to induction. For real $k_\parallel$ and time dependence $e^{-i\omega t}$, elimination gives
$$
\omega^2+i\eta_m k_\parallel^2\omega-v_A^2k_\parallel^2=0,
\qquad
\omega=-\frac{i\eta_m k_\parallel^2}{2}\pm\sqrt{v_A^2k_\parallel^2-\frac{\eta_m^2k_\parallel^4}{4}}.
$$
Thus weak <magnetic diffusion>, $\eta_m|k_\parallel|\ll v_A$, gives an <amplitude> decay rate $\eta_m k_\parallel^2/2$. For $\eta_m|k_\parallel|>2v_A$, both temporal roots are purely decaying; they no longer describe an oscillating traveling mode. This temporal calculation at fixed real <wavenumber> is different from a driven vessel at fixed real <frequency>, where a complex spatial <wavenumber> describes penetration and attenuation. <Viscosity> also dissipates <kinetic energy>, and walls introduce transverse gradients and additional losses.

For an axial length $L$, comparing the <magnetic diffusion> time $L^2/\eta_m$ with the transit time $L/v_A$ gives the ratio $Lv_A/\eta_m$. A large value favors propagation before diffusion, but a narrow vessel can introduce a shorter transverse diffusion scale. In the actual 1949 analysis, finite <electrical conductivity> and vessel dimensions restrict the useful <frequency> interval; above its upper limit the response becomes a skin wave.<Experimental Investigations of Magneto-Hydrodynamic Waves by Stig Lundquist>[Abstract: finite-conductivity frequency interval, upper limit and geometrical lower limit]{ref}

= Lundquist liquid-metal Alfvén wave experiment
{c}
{parent=Alfvén wave}
{title2=1949}

<Stig Lundquist>, the author printed as S. Lundquist, reported an early laboratory test in 1949. The apparatus used <mercury (element)> in a stainless-steel cylinder, a vibrating disk, and a floating mirror to indicate the motion at the liquid surface. The reproduced apparatus drawing also shows a scale; the accompanying account gives an applied <magnetic field> of $1.3\,\mathrm T$.<Numerical investigations of rotating MHD in a spherical shell by Andrey Sheyko>[Andrey Sheyko, Numerical investigations of rotating MHD in a spherical shell (2014), p. 8, Figure 1.4 and following paragraph; reproduction of Lundquist's apparatus]{ref}

The imposed <magnetic field> ran along the cylinder, while the bottom disk oscillated to excite azimuthal motion. Varying the drive <frequency> sought a <standing wave> <resonance>. A later authored experimental account describes this as an early hint: the <resonance> peak was not reached, and magnetic damping was substantial.<Torsional Alfvén waves in a dipolar magnetic field: experiments and simulations by Z. Tigrine and others>[2019, Section 1, paragraph beginning “Only a few laboratory experiments”: mercury vessel, bottom crenelated disk, resonance search and unreached peak]{ref} The experiment therefore tested a bounded azimuthal shear disturbance, related to a <torsional Alfvén wave>, rather than an infinite <plane wave> or a compression-driven <magnetosonic wave>.

\Image[alfven-wave-liquid-mercury-test.png]
{title=Functional chain of Lundquist's early mercury Alfvén-wave test}
{description=An original functional schematic of the <mercury (element)> vessel, axial <magnetic field>, bottom disk excitation and surface mirror readout. It is not a dimensional reconstruction. The <frequency> scan sought a <standing wave> <resonance>; the early experiment did not establish a resolved peak.}

The <magnetic field> provides the controlled physical parameter: the ideal <Alfvén wave> prediction makes a propagation speed proportional to its strength, while a <resonance> also depends on the vessel's <boundary conditions>. A quantitative test must compare the driven response with finite-<electrical conductivity> theory and account for mechanical coupling and wall losses. An observed surface motion alone cannot distinguish all these mechanisms. The reproduced apparatus account gives neither a numerical drive <frequency> nor a field-off comparison. Lundquist's paper itself calls the experiment preliminary and proposes further investigations.<Experimental Investigations of Magneto-Hydrodynamic Waves by Stig Lundquist>[Abstract, final sentence: preliminary experiment and proposed investigations]{ref}

= Alfvén-resonant vorticity in a parallel magnetic shear flow
{parent=Alfvén wave}
{c}
{title2=$[\rho S^2-k_x^2B^2](\zeta-k_yU'w/S)=0$}

For an incompressible <shear flow> $U(z)\mathbf e_x$ with uniform <magnetic field> $B\mathbf e_x$, define $S=\sigma+k_xU$ for time dependence $e^{i\sigma t}$ and $\zeta=ik_xv-ik_yu$. Eliminating the magnetic perturbations from the horizontal <momentum> equations gives the displayed product for $S\ne0$. Away from the <Alfvén wave> resonance, it yields $\zeta=k_yU'w/S$. At resonance one must retain the original system: for constant $U$, $k_y=0$, $S^2=k_x^2B^2/\rho$, any smooth transverse amplitude $v(z)$ with $b_y=k_xBv/S$ and all other amplitudes zero is an <Alfvén wave>. Its nonzero <vorticity> disproves the unrestricted zero-vorticity reduction. Exponentially growing modes with real background coefficients cannot encounter these real-frequency resonances.

= Kinetic Alfvén wave
{parent=Alfvén wave}

A kinetic Alfvén wave is a dispersive extension of the <Alfvén wave> in a <plasma> when perpendicular kinetic or two-fluid effects, including a pressure response, matter. Its particular <dispersion relation> depends on the ordering and plasma parameters. A similar product of parallel and perpendicular wavenumbers can occur in a <whistler wave>, but agreement of a scaling power does not identify the underlying physical equations.

= Magnetohydrodynamic phase mixing
{parent=Alfvén wave}
{title2=$\partial_R f(R,z+v(R)t)=f_R+t v'(R)f_z$}

Neighboring magnetic surfaces with different <Alfvén speeds> carry the same axial waveform at different rates. For a traveling profile $f(R,z+v(R)t)$, differentiation produces the displayed term, so transverse gradients grow even without axial wave steepening. Small <magnetic diffusivity> or <viscosity> can eventually act strongly on these gradients. The ideal process redistributes phase rather than directly dissipating wave energy.

= Torsional Alfvén wave
{parent=Alfvén wave}
{title2=$\rho R^2\mu_0\partial_t^2\Omega=\mathbf B_p\cdot\nabla(R^2\mathbf B_p\cdot\nabla\Omega)$}

A torsional <Alfvén wave> couples azimuthal <velocity> to a <toroidal magnetic field> around a fixed <poloidal magnetic field>. Axisymmetric induction gives $\partial_tB_\phi=R\mathbf B_p\cdot\nabla\Omega$ and magnetic tension gives $\rho R^2\mu_0\partial_t\Omega=\mathbf B_p\cdot\nabla(RB_\phi)$. Their combination is the displayed wave equation. Its local <dispersion relation> is $\omega^2=(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf B_p)^2/(\mu_0\rho)$.

= Pressure compatibility of nonlinear torsional Alfvén profiles
{parent=Torsional Alfvén wave}
{title2=$\partial_z(fg)=0$}

For uniform-density <incompressible flow> with $u_\phi=f(R,z+vt)+g(R,z-vt)$, $B_\phi/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}=f-g$ and $B_z/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}=v(R)$, the azimuthal <magnetohydrodynamic momentum equation> and <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> hold automatically. Let $\Pi=p/\rho+\Phi+[(f-g)^2+v^2]/2$. The remaining equations require $\Pi_z=0$ and $\Pi_R=4fg/R$, so $\partial_z(fg)=0$ is necessary and locally sufficient. A single traveling profile is unrestricted; two nonzero profiles on an unrestricted axial domain must be opposite exponentials or independent of their traveling arguments. Bounded profiles of both signs therefore reduce to steady solutions.

= Cylinder-averaged torsional Alfvén wave
{parent=Torsional Alfvén wave}
{title2=$\partial_t^2V=\frac{1}{2z_b\mu_0\rho r^3}\partial_r(r^3H\partial_rV)$}

For a rotating conducting sphere, a geostrophic azimuthal <velocity> $U=rV$ couples to the cylinder-integrated magnetic stress $T=\int B_rB_\phi\,dz$. Put $H=\int B_r^2\,dz$ and let $2z_b$ be the cylinder height. With an insulating boundary and no flow through it, cylindrical averaging removes the <Coriolis force> and gives $U_t=(2z_b\mu_0\rho r^2)^{-1}\partial_r(r^2T)$, $T_t=Hr\partial_r(U/r)$. The local squared wave speed is $H/(2z_b\mu_0\rho)$, the cylinder average of $B_r^2/(\mu_0\rho)$.

= Conserved energy of a cylinder-averaged torsional wave
{parent=Cylinder-averaged torsional Alfvén wave}
{title2=$E=\frac12\int(2z_b\mu_0\rho U^2+T^2/H)r\,dr$}

For a <cylinder-averaged torsional Alfvén wave> with time-independent $H>0$, differentiating this quadratic form gives $dE/dt=[rUT]_0^a$. Regularity at the axis and vanishing magnetic torque at the outer endpoint therefore imply conservation. By <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality>, $T^2/H\leq\int B_\phi^2\,dz$; equality holds for the torsional-wave magnetic component proportional to $B_r$ along each cylinder. A stationary magnetic component orthogonal to $B_r$ is invisible to $T$.

= Magnetic axial angular momentum flux
{parent=Torsional Alfvén wave}
{title2=$\mathbf F_{J,p}=-RB_\phi\mathbf B_p/\mu_0$}

The <magnetic tension> stress transports axial <angular momentum> along a <poloidal magnetic field>. In purely azimuthal ideal flow the poloidal energy flux is $\mathbf F_{E,p}=\Omega\mathbf F_{J,p}$. This proportionality is valid even at vanishing flux, where an ordinary ratio is undefined. The coupled fluxes explain how a <torsional Alfvén wave> redistributes rotation.

= Nonlinear Alfvén wave
{parent=Alfvén wave}
{title2=$v_s=u_x+sB_x/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}$}

A finite-amplitude ideal-MHD Alfvén wave can rotate the transverse <magnetic field> at constant magnitude, with constant <mass density>, <pressure> and longitudinal components. Its transverse velocity obeys $\mathbf u_\perp=\mathbf C-s\mathbf B_\perp/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}$ and its speed is constant along the wave. The constant <magnetic pressure> prevents compressive acceleration.

= Magnetic-pressure obstruction to a linearly polarized Alfvén wave
{parent=Nonlinear Alfvén wave}

A finite sinusoidal transverse field $B_x=aB_0\cos[k(z-v_at)]$, $B_y=0$, has varying <magnetic pressure>. In a purely transverse traveling <Alfvén wave>, longitudinal momentum requires $\partial_z[p+B_x^2/(2\mu_0)]=0$, but the adiabatic <pressure> equation with $u_z=0$ requires $\partial_t p=0$. A traveling compensating gas <pressure> cannot satisfy both conditions. The uncompensated force is quadratic in amplitude and drives a compressive response. A regular periodic one-dimensional traveling solution cannot evade this by varying <mass density>: its mass, transverse momentum and induction integrals force constant <mass density> at the zeros of the transverse field and hence throughout the nontrivial wave.

= Circularly polarized nonlinear Alfvén wave
{parent=Nonlinear Alfvén wave}
{title2=$\mathbf u_\perp=-\mathbf B_\perp/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho_0}$}

In homogeneous <ideal magnetohydrodynamics>, a constant longitudinal field $B_0>0$ and transverse field $aB_0(\cos\zeta,\sin\zeta)$, $\zeta=k(z-v_at)$, give an exact traveling wave with constant <mass density> and <pressure>, $u_z=0$, $v_a=B_0/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho_0}$ and the displayed transverse <velocity>. Induction and transverse momentum reduce to the same wave relation. Since $|\mathbf B_\perp|^2=a^2B_0^2$ is constant, there is no longitudinal <magnetic pressure> <gradient>. All nonlinear terms are compatible with the constant-density solution, even in a compressible fluid.

= Alfvén characteristic eigenvector
{c}
{parent=Alfvén wave}
{title2=$r_{A,s}$}

For propagation along $x$, the Alfvén right <eigenvector> has $\delta\rho=\delta p=\delta u_x=\delta B_x=0$, $\mathbf B_\perp\cdot\delta\mathbf u_\perp=0$ and $\delta\mathbf B_\perp=-s\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}\,\delta\mathbf u_\perp$. Its speed is $u_x+sB_x/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}$, $s=\pm1$. When the transverse background field vanishes, both transverse polarizations are allowed.

= Alfvén frequency
{title2=$\omega_a=\mathbf k\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}$}
{c}
{parent=Alfvén wave}

The Alfvén frequency is the frequency of an <Alfvén wave> with <wavevector> $\mathbf k$ in a uniform <magnetic field> $\mathbf B$ and <mass density> $\rho$. For a vertical field and vertical <wavenumber> $k$, its square is $\omega_a^2=k^2B_z^2/(\mu_0\rho)$. Its competition with orbital shear determines the <ideal magnetorotational dispersion relation>.

= Alfvén wing
{c}
{parent=Alfvén wave}

An Alfvén wing is a stationary Alfvénic disturbance attached to a conducting obstacle moving through a magnetized plasma. In a super-Alfvénic flow its boundaries follow downstream characteristics.

= Alfvénic Mach-cone slope
{title2=$m$}
{c}
{parent=Alfvén wing}

For a stationary cold-plasma disturbance with obstacle speed $u_0>u_A$, the characteristic slope is $m=\sqrt{u_0^2/u_A^2-1}$. It approaches $u_0/u_A$ in the strongly super-Alfvénic limit.

= Magnetosonic wave
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic wave}
{wiki}

= Magnetoacoustic wave
{synonym}

A magnetosonic wave is a compressive MHD mode polarized in the plane of the wavevector and background magnetic field.

= High-pressure limit of magnetosonic waves
{parent=Magnetosonic wave}
{title2=$v_s^2\gg v_a^2$}

= High-beta magnetosonic limit
{synonym}

For <sound speed> $v_s$ much larger than <Alfvén speed> $v_a$, and angle $\theta$ between the <wavevector> and <magnetic field>, the <fast magnetosonic wave> has $v_f^2=v_s^2+v_a^2\sin^2\theta+O(v_a^4/v_s^2)$. The <slow magnetosonic wave> has $v_{\rm slow}^2=v_a^2\cos^2\theta[1-(v_a^2/v_s^2)\sin^2\theta+O(v_a^4/v_s^4)]$. The fast mode is predominantly longitudinal sound. The slow mode becomes almost incompressible, polarized in the plane of the <wavevector> and <magnetic field>; the <Alfvén wave> is polarized perpendicular to that plane. The latter two are predominantly magnetic-tension waves with equal leading <phase speeds>.

= Pseudo-Alfvén wave
{parent=High-pressure limit of magnetosonic waves}
{title2=$\omega^2=(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf v_a)^2$}

The high-<pressure> limit of the <slow magnetosonic wave> approaches an incompressible polarization in the plane of the background <magnetic field> and <wavevector>, perpendicular to the latter. It has the displayed leading <dispersion relation>, the same as the out-of-plane <Alfvén wave>. <Magnetic tension> is the restoring force, and <pressure> balances the compressive component of the magnetic force. In exactly incompressible homogeneous <ideal magnetohydrodynamics> it is one of the two transverse magnetic polarizations.

= Isothermal coplanar magnetohydrodynamic characteristic matrix
{parent=Magnetosonic wave}
{title2=$v=u_x\pm c_f,\ u_x\pm c_{\rm slow}$}

For one-dimensional flow obeying an <isothermal equation of state> with $u_z=B_z=0$, the primitive variables $(\rho,u_x,u_y,B_y)$ form a four-variable hyperbolic system and $B_x$ is constant. The speeds relative to $u_x$ satisfy $c^4-(c_s^2+v_a^2)c^2+c_s^2v_{ax}^2=0$. These are the fast and slow <magnetosonic waves>. The independent out-of-plane <Alfvén wave> polarization and a freely advected entropy mode are absent from this reduced isothermal system.

= Magnetosonic critical speed
{parent=Magnetosonic wave}
{title2=$c_{\mathrm f},c_{\mathrm s}$}

For propagation in a direction with field component $v_{an}$ of the <Alfvén velocity>, the squared <fast magnetosonic wave> and <slow magnetosonic wave> speeds are the roots of $c^4-(c_s^2+v_a^2)c^2+c_s^2v_{an}^2=0$. They are real and nonnegative, and satisfy $c_{\mathrm s}^2\leq v_{an}^2\leq c_{\mathrm f}^2$. A steady acceleration equation can become singular when the flow equals either of these speeds.

= Regularity at a magnetosonic point
{parent=Magnetosonic critical speed}

If a steady wind equation has the form $(w^2-c_{\mathrm f}^2)(w^2-c_{\mathrm s}^2)w'/w=\mathcal N$, a smooth nondegenerate crossing requires $\mathcal N=0$ at the corresponding <magnetosonic critical speed>. Subsequent differentiation or the full system determines the admissible slope. The numerator condition alone does not prove that a global smooth wind exists.

= Cold-limit degeneracy of a slow magnetosonic point
{parent=Regularity at a magnetosonic point}
{title2=$c_{\rm slow}=0$}

In a strictly cold flow governed by <ideal magnetohydrodynamics> the <sound speed> vanishes, so the <slow magnetosonic wave> speed is zero. A nonzero-speed outflow therefore has no ordinary finite interior slow crossing. For the <cold radial magnetohydrodynamic wind integral>, $f_y=-\alpha(x-x^{-1})^2/(y-1)^3-\beta/(x^4y^3)<0$ on the sub-Alfvénic branch $y>1$, assuming $\beta>0$. A small retained <specific enthalpy> is needed to locate a finite slow point; the zero-temperature limit can move it to the launch boundary. The usual simultaneous numerator and denominator regularity conditions still apply to nondegenerate critical points.

= Fast magnetosonic wave
{parent=Magnetosonic wave}
{wiki}

The fast magnetosonic mode uses gas and magnetic pressure as reinforcing restoring forces and is the faster root of the magnetosonic dispersion relation.

= Slow magnetosonic wave
{parent=Magnetosonic wave}
{wiki}

= Slow mode
{synonym}

= Slow wave
{synonym}

The slow magnetosonic mode is the lower-frequency compressive root and is guided more strongly along the magnetic field.

= Tube speed
{title2=$v_T$}
{parent=Magnetosonic wave}
{wiki}

The tube speed combines sound and Alfvén speeds according to
$$
v_T^2=\frac{v_s^2v_A^2}{v_s^2+v_A^2}.
$$
It is smaller than both constituent speeds.

= Magnetohydrodynamic interface wave
{title2=MHD interface wave}
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic wave}

An MHD interface wave propagates along a discontinuity and decays away from it. Continuity of normal displacement and total pressure determines its dispersion relation.

= Magnetic stabilization of an equal-density vortex sheet
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic interface wave}
{title2=$\sigma=-k_x\overline U\pm k_x\sqrt{v_A^2-(\Delta U)^2/4}$}

For two incompressible streams of equal density with a common tangential <magnetic field> parallel to their velocities, decaying <normal modes> and interface <displacement>/total-pressure continuity give $\rho(\sigma+k_xU_1)^2+\rho(\sigma+k_xU_2)^2=2k_x^2B^2$ in units $\mu_0=1$. The displayed <dispersion relation> follows, with $v_A^2=B^2/\rho$. Thus $|\Delta U|<2v_A$ excludes exponential <Kelvin-Helmholtz instability>; equality is marginal. Above threshold the <growth rate> is $|k_x|\sqrt{(\Delta U)^2/4-v_A^2}$. The transverse horizontal <wavenumber> affects the decay length through $k=(k_x^2+k_y^2)^{1/2}$ but not this growth threshold. <Magnetic tension> supplies the stabilizing restoring force.

= Force-free magnetic field
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}
{wiki}

A force-free magnetic field obeys $(\nabla\times\mathbf B)\times\mathbf B=0$, so its electric current is locally parallel to the magnetic field.

= Magnetic-energy injection by differential boundary rotation
{parent=Force-free magnetic field}
{title2=$\dot E_B=\frac{2\pi}{\mu_0}\int f(\psi)(\Omega_{\rm out}-\Omega_{\rm in})\,d\psi$}

The general ideal magnetic-energy balance has outward-surface contribution $\mu_0^{-1}\oint[(u\cdot B)B-B^2u]\cdot dS$ and volume contribution $-\int u\cdot(J\times B)dV$. The latter vanishes for a <force-free magnetic field>. For an axisymmetric boundary rotating tangentially, $u\cdot B=\Omega f(\psi)$ and the directed flux of a thin tube is $2\pi d\psi$. Pair its exit and entry endpoints to obtain the displayed energy injection, counting each boundary endpoint once. Equal endpoint angular <velocities> inject no <magnetic energy>.

= Force-free parameter
{title2=$\alpha$}
{parent=Force-free magnetic field}

Writing $\nabla\times\mathbf B=(4\pi/c)\alpha\mathbf B$ defines the force-free parameter $\alpha$. Since the divergence of a curl vanishes and $\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B=0$, it satisfies $\mathbf B\mathbin\cdot\nabla\alpha=0$.

= Cylindrical force-free magnetic field
{parent=Force-free magnetic field}

A cylindrical force-free magnetic field depends only on cylindrical radius and has current everywhere parallel to its magnetic field. Regularity on the axis often forces its radial component to vanish.

= Linearized ideal magnetohydrodynamic equations
{title2=linearized ideal MHD equations}
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

The linearized ideal magnetohydrodynamic equations retain terms first order in perturbations about a chosen ideal-MHD background. Around a uniform state they describe <Alfvén waves> and <magnetosonic waves>.

= Displacement equation for a parallel magnetic shear flow
{parent=Linearized ideal magnetohydrodynamic equations}
{title2=$[(\rho S^2-k_x^2B^2)f']'-k^2(\rho S^2-k_x^2B^2)f=0$}

For nonresonant incompressible <normal modes> of a uniform-density <shear flow> with aligned uniform <magnetic field>, let $S=\sigma+k_xU(z)$, $k^2=k_x^2+k_y^2$ and $f=w/S$. The normal <displacement> is $f/i$. The linearized horizontal equations and incompressibility give $ik^2p_1=\rho(Sw'-S'w)=\rho S^2f'$. Substituting the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> into normal <momentum> gives the displayed conservative equation. Across a zero-thickness <shear layer>, continuity of $f$ and of $(\rho S^2-k_x^2B^2)f'$ supplies the interface conditions. They express common normal <displacement> and continuous perturbed <magnetohydrodynamic total pressure>, avoiding undefined products of distributional shear gradients.

= Ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation
{title2=ideal MHD induction equation}
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

= MHD induction equation
{synonym}
{c}

The ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation is $\partial_t\mathbf B=\nabla\times(\mathbf u\times\mathbf B)$. Together with $\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B=0$, it implies magnetic-flux freezing.

= Ideal magnetic response to a cylindrical cyclonic event
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation}
{title2=$A=B_0r\sin(\phi-ft),\quad B_z=B_0tg'\cos(\phi-ft)$}

For an axisymmetric velocity $\mathbf u=(0,rf(r),g(r))$ independent of $z$, write $\mathbf B=B_z\widehat{\mathbf z}+\nabla\times(A\widehat{\mathbf z})$. Ideal induction becomes
$$
(\partial_t+f\partial_\phi)A=0,\qquad(\partial_t+f\partial_\phi)B_z=\frac{g'}r\partial_\phi A.
$$
The first equation expresses <magnetic flux freezing> of the transverse flux function, while the second is axial stretching $B_rg'$. Starting from a uniform field $B_0\widehat{\mathbf x}$ gives $A(r,\phi,t)=B_0r\sin(\phi-f(r)t)$ and $B_z(r,\phi,t)=B_0t g'(r)\cos(\phi-f(r)t)$. In complex azimuthal-mode notation, $\widehat A=B_0r e^{-ift}$ and $\widehat B_z=iB_0t g'e^{-ift}$, obtained by elementary integrating factors. The transverse field has $B_r=r^{-1}A_\phi$ and $B_\phi=-A_r$, preserving zero <divergence>.

= Cartesian magnetic flux function
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation}
{title2=$\psi$}

For a $y$-independent solenoidal <magnetic field>, the transverse components can be written $B_x=-\psi_z$ and $B_z=\psi_x$. Contours of $\psi$ are transverse <magnetic field lines>. The independent axial component $B_y$ can twist the <flux tube>. Under the <MHD induction equation>, an additive time-dependent gauge can be chosen so that $D\psi/Dt=0$.

= Magnetic island
{parent=Cartesian magnetic flux function}

A magnetic island in a planar <magnetic field> is a region of closed <magnetic field lines> surrounding an extremum of the <Cartesian magnetic flux function>. A surrounding <separatrix> can pass through a saddle of that function and separate the island from other flux regions. In ideal <incompressible flow>, the flux-function value carried by each fluid particle and the area enclosed by each material contour are conserved.

= Cartesian magnetostatic flux-function equilibrium
{parent=Cartesian magnetic flux function}

In a $y$-independent <magnetostatic equilibrium> with no axial pressure or gravitational force, $\nabla\psi\times\nabla B_y=0$. Thus $B_y=b(\psi)$ on a regular connected flux region. The remaining balance is $[\nabla^2\psi+bb']\nabla\psi/\mu_0+\nabla p+\rho\nabla\Phi=0$. Constancy on separate components of a level set does not automatically give one global single-valued $b$.

= Axisymmetric magnetic winding
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation}
{title2=$\partial_tB_\phi=R\mathbf B_p\cdot\nabla\Omega$}

For prescribed axisymmetric <differential rotation> $\mathbf u=R\Omega\mathbf e_\phi$, the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> and <Gauss's law for magnetism> give
$$
\partial_t\mathbf B_p=0,\qquad\partial_tB_\phi=R\mathbf B_p\cdot\nabla\Omega.
$$
The poloidal field is unchanged while rotational shear generates a <toroidal magnetic field>. For time-independent $\Omega$, the toroidal component grows linearly until neglected dynamical feedback becomes relevant.

= Magnetic-pressure equality radius under Keplerian winding
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetic winding}
{title2=$R_{\rm eq}\propto t^{2/3}$}

For <Keplerian rotation>, initial $B_R\propto R^{-1}$ and thermal pressure $p\propto R^{-2}$, <axisymmetric magnetic winding> gives $B_\phi=-(3/2)\Omega tB_R$. If the initial <plasma beta> is $\beta_p>1$, the formal equality of <magnetic pressure> and gas pressure occurs at
$$
R_{\rm eq}^3=\frac{9GMt^2}{4(\beta_p-1)}.
$$
The magnetic-to-thermal pressure ratio decreases with radius, so magnetic pressure dominates inside this radius. In a disk with a finite radial range, an equality radius exists in the disk only when this formal radius lies within that range.

= Ferraro's law of isorotation
{c}
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetic winding}
{title2=$\mathbf B_p\cdot\nabla\Omega=0$}

A stationary axisymmetric <magnetic field> in ideal purely rotational flow requires constant angular velocity along the <magnetic field lines> of its <poloidal magnetic field>. Otherwise <axisymmetric magnetic winding> generates a time-dependent toroidal component.

= Entropy advection equation
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

For an adiabatic ideal fluid, entropy is materially conserved away from shocks: $Ds/Dt=0$. For a perfect gas this is equivalent to $Dp/Dt+\gamma p\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf u=0$.

= Magnetohydrodynamic shock
{title2=MHD shock}
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

A magnetohydrodynamic shock is a discontinuity satisfying conservation of mass, momentum, energy, normal magnetic field, and tangential electric field.

= Perpendicular magnetohydrodynamic shock
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic shock}
{title2=$B_2/B_1=\rho_2/\rho_1$}

For a planar <magnetohydrodynamic shock> with normal <velocity> and tangential <magnetic field>, <mass conservation> gives $\rho u=m$ and the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> gives $uB$ constant across the front. Hence $B/\rho$ is unchanged, expressing <magnetic flux freezing>. Normal <momentum> flux is $\rho u^2+p+B^2/(2\mu_0)$, and energy flux per unit mass flux is $u^2/2+\gamma p/[(\gamma-1)\rho]+B^2/(\mu_0\rho)$, apart from a smooth common <gravitational potential>. The amplified tangential field contributes both <magnetic pressure> and <magnetic energy> transport.

= Compression ratio of a perpendicular magnetohydrodynamic shock
{parent=Perpendicular magnetohydrodynamic shock}
{title2=$x=\rho_2/\rho_1$}

Put $c_1^2=\gamma p_1/\rho_1$ and $v_{A1}^2=B_1^2/(\mu_0\rho_1)$. The mass, <momentum> and energy jump conditions for a <perpendicular magnetohydrodynamic shock> factor into the unchanged state $x=1$ and
$$
(2-\gamma)v_{A1}^2x^2+[(\gamma-1)u_1^2+2c_1^2+\gamma v_{A1}^2]x-(\gamma+1)u_1^2=0.
$$
For the compressive physical branch $x>1$, positive downstream <pressure> implies $x<(\gamma+1)/(\gamma-1)$ when $\gamma>1$ and the upstream <sound speed> or <Alfvén speed> is nonzero. Indeed the polynomial implies
$$
\frac{p_2}{\rho_1}=
\frac{c_1^2[(\gamma+1)x-(\gamma-1)]/\gamma
+(\gamma-1)v_{A1}^2(x-1)^3/2}
{(\gamma+1)-(\gamma-1)x}.
$$
It then gives $u_1^2>c_1^2+v_{A1}^2$: the upstream flow exceeds the perpendicular fast <magnetosonic wave> speed. The exactly cold unmagnetized strong-shock limit reaches the upper compression bound.

= Parallel magnetohydrodynamic shock
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic shock}

In a planar <magnetohydrodynamic shock> with both <velocity> and <magnetic field> normal to the front, the normal <magnetic field> is continuous. Its normal <Maxwell stress tensor> contribution is consequently the same on both sides, and the <Poynting vector> vanishes because $\mathbf u\times\mathbf B=0$. The remaining jump conditions are the ordinary <Rankine-Hugoniot conditions for a perfect gas>. This is the purely parallel branch; an oblique or switch-on shock has additional transverse fields and is not covered by this reduction.

= Ideal magnetohydrodynamic shock conditions
{title2=ideal MHD shock conditions}
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic shock}

The ideal magnetohydrodynamic shock conditions are the Rankine-Hugoniot jump conditions for mass, momentum, energy, and magnetic flux. For a dynamically weak magnetic field, the normal field is continuous while the tangential field is multiplied by the gas compression ratio.

= de Hoffmann–Teller frame
{c}
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic shock}

The de Hoffmann-Teller frame is a tangentially boosted shock frame in which the electric field vanishes. Ideal MHD then makes the fluid velocity parallel to the magnetic field on both sides.

= Rotational discontinuity in magnetohydrodynamics
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic shock}

A rotational discontinuity changes the tangential direction of the magnetic field and velocity while leaving density, pressure, and normal components continuous. The normal flow is Alfvénic.

= Axisymmetric magnetostatic Grad-Shafranov system
{c}
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

For an axisymmetric vector potential $\mathbf A=\nabla\alpha\times\nabla\phi+\beta\nabla\phi$, define
$$
L=-r\partial_r(r^{-1}\partial_r)-\partial_z^2.
$$
Magnetostatic azimuthal force balance gives $L\alpha=F(\beta)$. In a barotropic equilibrium the remaining balance integrates to
$$
L\beta=F(\beta)F'(\beta)+r^2\rho G(\beta)
$$
for flux functions $F$ and $G$.

= Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind
{title2=axisymmetric MHD wind}
{parent=Ideal magnetohydrodynamics}

A steady axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind follows nested magnetic-flux surfaces and has conserved mass loading, field-line angular velocity, angular momentum, entropy, and Bernoulli quantities along each surface.

= Cold radial magnetohydrodynamic wind integral
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}
{title2=$f(x,y)=\mathrm{constant}$}

For a cold equatorial <axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind> with $B_p=CR^{-2}$ in the gravity of a <point mass> $M$, smooth crossing of the <Alfvén surface> gives $\rho_a=\mu_0k^2$ and $\ell=\omega R_a^2$. Put $x=R/R_a$, $y=\rho/\rho_a$, $\alpha=\omega^2R_a^3/(GM)$ and $\beta=C^2/(\mu_0\rho_a GM R_a^3)$. The corotating <energy>, scaled by $GM/R_a$, is
$$
f=\frac\alpha2\left[\frac{(x-x^{-1})^2}{(y-1)^2}-x^2\right]+\frac\beta{2x^4y^2}-\frac1x.
$$
The azimuthal <velocity> relative to field-line rotation is $\omega R_a(x-x^{-1})/(y-1)$ and the poloidal speed is $C/(\sqrt{\mu_0\rho_a}R_a^2x^2y)$. The apparent singularity at $x=y=1$ is interpreted through a smooth limiting slope. A nondegenerate magnetosonic crossing on a constant-$f$ curve requires $f_x=f_y=0$ and a real admissible tangent slope.

= Sub-Alfvénic isorotation in a steady axisymmetric wind
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}
{title2=$\Omega\simeq\Omega_F\quad(M_{Ap}^2\ll1)$}

Assume zero toroidal loop voltage in smooth steady <ideal magnetohydrodynamics>, so the poloidal flow aligns with the <poloidal magnetic field>. Write $\rho\mathbf u_p=\eta\mathbf B_p$. The <continuity equation> makes $\eta$ constant along a magnetic line. The induction equation gives the conserved <field-line angular velocity> $\Omega_F=\Omega-\eta B_\phi/(\rho R)$. The azimuthal momentum equation is $\rho\mathbf u_p\cdot\nabla(R^2\Omega)=\mathbf B_p\cdot\nabla(RB_\phi)/\mu_0$; hence $L=R^2\Omega-RB_\phi/(\mu_0\eta)$ is also constant along the line.

With $M_{Ap}^2=\mu_0\eta^2/\rho=|\mathbf u_p|^2/v_{Ap}^2$, eliminating $B_\phi$ gives
$$
\Omega=\frac{\Omega_F-M_{Ap}^2L/R^2}{1-M_{Ap}^2}.
$$
For $M_{Ap}^2\ll1$ and nonsingular invariants, $\Omega=\Omega_F+O(M_{Ap}^2)$ and matter approximately corotates along a field line. With exactly zero poloidal flow, induction directly gives <Ferraro's law of isorotation>. The approximation requires the correction $M_{Ap}^2(\Omega_F-L/R^2)$ to be small on the segment in question.

= Corotating energy invariant of an axisymmetric magnetic wind
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}
{title2=$\epsilon_J=|\mathbf u|^2/2+\Phi-\omega Ru_\phi$}

For a pressure-free flowing <poloidal flux surface>, subtracting its angular <velocity> times the azimuthal momentum equation from the mechanical energy equation shows that $\epsilon_J$ is constant. It equals poloidal kinetic energy plus $(u_\phi-R\omega)^2/2+\Phi-R^2\omega^2/2$. This is the total <magnetohydrodynamic Bernoulli invariant> minus $\omega$ times the total <magnetohydrodynamic angular-momentum invariant>, not the total energy alone.

= Field-line angular velocity of an axisymmetric wind
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}
{title2=$\mathbf u=k\mathbf B/\rho+R\omega(\psi)\mathbf e_\phi$}

In steady axisymmetric <ideal magnetohydrodynamics>, induction makes $\omega$ constant on regular connected <poloidal flux surfaces>. It describes field-line rotation. The material angular <velocity> is $u_\phi/R=\omega+kB_\phi/(\rho R)$ and differs when the toroidal <magnetic field> contributes. If the <poloidal magnetic field> vanishes, the flux-surface integration requires separate treatment.

= Poloidal magnetic flux function
{title2=$\psi$}
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}

= Axisymmetric magnetic flux function
{synonym}

For $\mathbf B_p=\nabla\psi\times\nabla\phi$, surfaces $\psi=\text{constant}$ are poloidal magnetic surfaces and $2\pi\psi$ is magnetic flux up to the choice of axial reference value.

= Material advection of an axisymmetric magnetic flux function
{parent=Poloidal magnetic flux function}
{title2=$D\Psi/Dt=0$}

For an axisymmetric <poloidal magnetic field> $\mathbf B_p=\nabla\Psi\times\nabla\phi$, the <ideal magnetohydrodynamic induction equation> gives $\partial_t\Psi+\mathbf u_p\cdot\nabla\Psi=0$, after fixing the time-dependent additive constant in $\Psi$. A regular axial reference makes $2\pi\Psi$ the enclosed <magnetic flux>. This scalar statement of <magnetic flux freezing> holds even when rotation also produces a <toroidal magnetic field>.

= Axisymmetric magnetic flux surface
{parent=Poloidal magnetic flux function}
{title2=$\psi=\mathrm{constant}$}

= Poloidal flux surface
{synonym}

A regular level surface of the axisymmetric <poloidal magnetic flux function> contains both the poloidal and toroidal <magnetic field> directions. Conservation along field lines can be expressed locally as a function of the flux label on connected regular surfaces. Degenerate points with no <poloidal magnetic field> and disconnected branches with the same label require separate treatment.

= Power-law poloidal field near a disk surface
{parent=Poloidal magnetic flux function}

If $\Psi(R,0)=\Psi_1+\Psi_0(R/R_0)^\delta$ and the surface inclination to the vertical is a radius-independent $\alpha$, then $B_z=\delta\Psi_0R^{\delta-2}/R_0^\delta$ and $B_R=B_z\tan\alpha$. The <solenoidal vector field> condition gives
$$
\left.\partial_zB_z\right|_{0}=-\frac{\delta-1}{R}B_z(R,0)\tan\alpha.
$$
The constant offset in the <poloidal magnetic flux function> has no effect on the <magnetic field>.

= Magnetohydrodynamic mass loading
{title2=$k(\psi)$}
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}

When $\rho\mathbf u_p=k\mathbf B_p$, the flux-surface function $k$ is the mass flux per unit poloidal magnetic flux.

= Regular-axis alignment of steady poloidal ideal flow
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic mass loading}
{title2=$\rho\mathbf u=k(\psi)\mathbf B$}

For steady axisymmetric ideal flow with only poloidal components, induction implies $\mathbf u\times\mathbf B=C\mathbf e_\phi/R$. Axis regularity, or zero toroidal <electromotive force>, sets $C=0$. Continuity then gives $\rho\mathbf u=k(\psi)\mathbf B$, locally on connected magnetic surfaces of the <poloidal magnetic flux function>. Without the zero-circulation condition, annular domains can support steady cross-field flow.

= Field-line angular velocity
{title2=$\omega(\psi)$}
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}

Ideal induction in a steady axisymmetric flow makes each magnetic surface rotate with a constant field-line angular velocity $\omega(\psi)$.

= Magnetohydrodynamic angular-momentum invariant
{title2=$\ell(\psi)$}
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}

The total specific angular momentum transported by matter and magnetic stress is
$$
\ell=ru_\phi-\frac{rB_\phi}{\mu_0k},
$$
and is constant on each magnetic surface.

= Maxwell torque conservation in an axisymmetric wind
{c}
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic angular-momentum invariant}
{title2=$\mathbf F_J=\rho\mathbf u_p s u_\phi-\mu_0^{-1}\mathbf B_p sB_\phi$}

In a steady <axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind>, axisymmetric <pressure> and <Newtonian gravity> exert no azimuthal <torque>. The azimuthal momentum equation is $\rho\mathbf u_p\cdot\nabla(su_\phi)=\mu_0^{-1}\mathbf B_p\cdot\nabla(sB_\phi)$. <Mass conservation> and the <solenoidal vector field> condition imply $\nabla\cdot\mathbf F_J=0$. With <magnetohydrodynamic mass loading> $\rho\mathbf u_p=\kappa\mathbf B_p$, the transported <angular momentum> per unit poloidal <magnetic flux> is $\ell=\kappa s u_\phi-sB_\phi/\mu_0$. It is constant on connected regular <poloidal flux surfaces>. The usual specific <magnetohydrodynamic angular-momentum invariant> is $L=\ell/\kappa$, provided $\kappa\ne0$; confusing these two normalizations loses a factor of mass loading in the wind <torque>.

= Magnetohydrodynamic Bernoulli invariant
{title2=$\epsilon(\psi)$}
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}

Along a steady axisymmetric ideal-MHD wind, the total specific energy
$$
\epsilon=\frac12|\mathbf u|^2+\Phi+h
-\frac{r\omega B_\phi}{\mu_0k}
$$
is constant on each magnetic surface. The last term is the electromagnetic energy transported per unit mass flux.

= Isothermal magnetic Bernoulli integral
{parent=Magnetohydrodynamic Bernoulli invariant}
{title2=$\epsilon(\psi)$}

In aligned steady ideal flow with $p=c_s^2\rho$ and constant <isothermal sound speed>, the <Lorentz force density> has no field-parallel component. Projecting momentum along the <magnetic field> makes $u^2/2+\Phi+c_s^2\ln(\rho/\rho_0)$ constant on each connected magnetic surface. The arbitrary reference density changes only the additive constant.

= Alfvén surface
{c}
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}
{wiki}

An Alfvén surface is where the poloidal flow speed equals the poloidal Alfvén speed. Smooth passage through it imposes a regularity condition relating field-line angular velocity and angular momentum.

= Alfvén radius
{c}
{title2=$R_A$}
{parent=Alfvén surface}

The Alfvén radius characterizes where a magnetized <stellar wind> crosses its <Alfvén surface>. For a roughly radial wind, $v_{\rm wind}\simeq B/\sqrt{\mu_0\rho}$ and $\dot M_w\simeq4\pi R_A^2\rho v_{\rm wind}$. The wind's angular-momentum lever arm is of order $R_A$: its torque is $\dot J\sim-\dot M_w\Omega R_A^2$, up to geometry factors. The precise wind invariant uses the cylindrical radius of the surface crossing, rather than necessarily its spherical radius.

= Alfvén-surface regularity condition for an axisymmetric wind
{c}
{parent=Alfvén surface}

If $r_A$ is the cylindrical radius at which $M_A^2=\mu_0k^2/\rho=1$, finite azimuthal velocity and magnetic field require
$$
\ell=\omega r_A^2.
$$
This cancels the simultaneous numerator and denominator zeros in the wind's azimuthal integrals.

= Asymptotic energy of a radial magnetohydrodynamic wind
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}

If $r^2|B_p|\to F$ and $u_p\to u_\infty$ along an open magnetic surface, then
$$
B_\phi\sim-\frac{\omega F}{u_\infty r},
\qquad
|v_{A\phi}|\to\omega\sqrt{\frac{F}{\mu_0ku_\infty}}.
$$
When enthalpy and gravity vanish asymptotically, the Bernoulli invariant becomes
$$
\epsilon=\frac12u_\infty^2+\frac{\omega^2F}{\mu_0ku_\infty}.
$$

= Magnetocentrifugal acceleration
{parent=Axisymmetric magnetohydrodynamic wind}

Magnetocentrifugal acceleration occurs when matter tied to a rotating inclined magnetic field moves outward under the effective centrifugal force. For a Keplerian disc, cold launching is possible when the field tilts by more than $30^\circ$ from the vertical.

= Thirty-degree magnetocentrifugal launching criterion
{parent=Magnetocentrifugal acceleration}
{title2=$\alpha>\pi/6$}

= Blandford-Payne launching criterion
{c}
{synonym}

For a cold corotating field line anchored in a circular point-mass orbit, the <effective potential> has meridional Hessian $\omega^2\operatorname{diag}(-3,1)$. Along a straight line inclined by $\alpha$ to the vertical its quadratic curvature is $\omega^2(1-4\sin^2\alpha)$. Negative curvature gives the strict local launching criterion. Equality is marginal and global escape requires additional information.

= Marginal straight-line launch at thirty degrees
{parent=Thirty-degree magnetocentrifugal launching criterion}
{title2=$\Delta\Phi_{\mathrm{eff}}/(GM/R_0)=-7(s/R_0)^3/16+O(s^4)$}

At exactly thirty degrees the quadratic curvature vanishes, but the specified straight-line point-mass <effective potential> is downhill for small positive displacement at cubic order. Thus the strict negative-curvature criterion does not exclude every nonlinear one-sided marginal launch. An exactly stationary bead remains at equilibrium until displaced.

= Magnetocentrifugal launching criterion in a flattened power-law potential
{parent=Magnetocentrifugal acceleration}
{title2=$\alpha_{\rm crit}=\arctan(|\lambda|/\sqrt{\beta+2})$}

In a <flattened power-law gravitational potential> $\Phi=\Phi_0R_0^\beta(R^2+\lambda^2z^2)^{-\beta/2}$, a magnetic surface corotating with its disk footpoint has an <effective potential> with meridional <Hessian matrix> $\Omega_f^2\operatorname{diag}[-(\beta+2),\lambda^2]$ there. A cold outward displacement along a field inclined by $\alpha$ to the vertical is downhill when $\tan^2\alpha>\lambda^2/(\beta+2)$. Equality is marginal and can depend on higher derivatives and field-line curvature; this is a local launching criterion, not a guarantee of a global escaping wind.
